+      # THE CONVERGENCE RULE, and the reason it is four steps of plumbing around
+      # one script rather than a paragraph in the prompt above.
+      #
+      # A re-review is a fresh review. This workflow reviews the head commit, so
+      # answering every finding produces a new head, a new diff and — necessarily
+      # — new findings. On 2026-08-24 four branches sat at rounds five, eight,
+      # ten and two: the findings shrank each round to a `§4.1` that should read
+      # `§2.4` and a line number that had drifted three lines, and
+      # `ai-review:blocking` stayed on. The orchestrator's rule is *merge once CI
+      # is green*, which never arrived. Issue #169.
+      #
+      # The rule could have been written into the prompt. It is not, because a
+      # rule a model is asked to apply to its own verdict is a hope, and because
+      # the round a finding was first seen has to come from somewhere the model
+      # cannot re-date. So: the reviewer publishes findings, this ledger records
+      # when each was first seen, and .github/scripts/review-verdict.sh — 87
+      # assertions, no network, no `gh` — decides the label from the two.
+      #
+      # Nothing here can weaken what blocks. A `floor` finding holds the pull
+      # request at every round, and every unreadable input is read in the
+      # direction that blocks.
+      - name: Read the ledger and this round's findings
+        id: ledger
+        # continue-on-error on all five, and it is the same argument the Review
+        # step above makes: this job's verdict is the LABEL, not the exit code,
+        # and a job that goes red because a `gh api` call timed out is a job
+        # people learn to ignore. Every one of these failing leaves the reviewer's
+        # own label standing, which is the behaviour this repository had before
+        # the rule existed -- so the failure mode of the convergence rule is the
+        # absence of the convergence rule, never a weaker one.
+        continue-on-error: true
+        if: >-
+          !cancelled() && steps.auth.outputs.ok == 'true' &&
+          steps.happened.outputs.ran == 'yes'
+        env:
+          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
+          REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
+          PR: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+          : > /tmp/prev-ledger.md
+          : > /tmp/findings.md
+
+          # Ids first, bodies second, and deliberately not `--paginate` with a
+          # reducing `--jq`: `map(...) | last` runs once per PAGE and emits one
+          # answer per page, so the answer silently changes when a pull request
+          # crosses a hundred comments. Selecting element-wise and taking the
+          # last id is the shape that survives that.
+          # .github/tests/gh-api-usage-test.sh is about exactly this class of
+          # call.
+          # `startswith`, NOT `contains`, and the difference is a comment this
+          # step would otherwise overwrite. The prompt above names this marker
+          # to the reviewer, so a reviewer explaining what it read can reproduce
+          # the marker inside its OWN comment -- which `contains` would then
+          # match, `tail -1` would prefer as the newer of the two, and the PATCH
+          # below would overwrite the review with the ledger. The ledger's body
+          # begins with the marker; a quotation of it never does.
+          ledger_id=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/issues/$PR/comments" --paginate \
+            --jq '.[] | select(.body | startswith("<!-- attadipa-review-ledger -->")) | .id' \
+            | tail -1)
+          if [ -n "$ledger_id" ]; then
+            gh api "repos/$REPO/issues/comments/$ledger_id" --jq '.body' \
+              > /tmp/prev-ledger.md
+            echo "ledger_id=$ledger_id" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
+          fi
+
+          # The reviewer edits its comment in place, so a stale block can be read
+          # as this round's. That is harmless and not worth guarding: re-applying
+          # last round's statements reconciles to the same state, which is what
+          # carrying forward already does when there is no block at all.
+          # `contains` here is correct -- the block is at the END of the
+          # reviewer's comment -- but the ledger is excluded by name, so a
+          # future ledger that quotes the findings marker cannot be read as a
+          # round's findings.
+          find_id=$(gh api "repos/$REPO/issues/$PR/comments" --paginate \
+            --jq '.[]
+                  | select((.body | startswith("<!-- attadipa-review-ledger -->")) | not)
+                  | select(.body | contains("<!-- attadipa-review-findings"))
+                  | .id' \
+            | tail -1)
